## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical understanding of MVCC anomalies, their manifestation under common PostgreSQL isolation levels, and concrete strategies to guarantee correctness in financial style workloads.

## Key Concepts
- MVCC, phantom reads, write skew
- Isolation levels: READ COMMITTED, REPEATABLE READ, SERIALIZABLE
- Locking: SELECT FOR UPDATE, advisory locks
- Integrity via constraints/triggers when needed

## Code Example
```sql
-- Reproduce under READ COMMITTED
BEGIN;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 'A';
-- T2 runs concurrently
SELECT SUM(balance) FROM accounts WHERE status='active';
COMMIT;
```

```sql
-- Safe fix
BEGIN;
SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id = 'A' FOR UPDATE;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 'A';
COMMIT;
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you measure SERIALIZABLE overhead in this workload?
- How would you adapt to a sharded setup with cross-shard risk calculation?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of MVCC, SSI, and how isolation levels affect correctness in concurrent money transfers.

## Key Concepts
- ACID under MVCC, SSI
- Row-level locking, SELECT ... FOR UPDATE
- Write skew prevention, overdraft safety

## Code Example
```sql
BEGIN;
SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id = :from FOR UPDATE;
SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id = :to FOR UPDATE;
IF (balance_from >= :amount) THEN
  UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - :amount WHERE id = :from;
  UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + :amount WHERE id = :to;
  INSERT INTO movements (...);
  COMMIT;
ELSE
  ROLLBACK;
END IF;
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test this under high contention? 
- What changes if you must support distributed transactions across shards?
